<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kilwa Kisiwani</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kilwa Kisiwani is an island off the southern coast of Tanzania, seat of the Kilwa Sultanate, which from the 13th century controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in 1331 and praised it as one of the most beautiful towns in the world. The Portuguese sacked it in 1505, and its ruins, including the Great Mosque and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husuni-kubwa">Husuni Kubwa</a>, form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kilwa-kisiwani.jpg" alt="Kilwa Kisiwani" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khalidsalewa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_Kilwa_Interior.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kilwa Kisiwani lies 9 degrees south of the equator, with a circumference of 23 km and a land area of 12 square kilometres. The Mavuji River estuary lies to the west, the Sagarungu sound to the south and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> to the east. The island has no rivers, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> have been used for over a thousand years. The topsoil over the limestone is poor, so food on land came from higher ground, while seafood dominated the diet; the soil of the region suited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, which was used for sails and for cloth. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> wind reversals shaped the trade of the island with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Radiocarbon dating has confirmed the founding of the settlement at the start of the 9th century CE. Local oral tradition says that in the 11th century the island was sold to Ali bin Hasan, a son of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a>, who married the daughter of the local king; research shows he arrived at an already inhabited place, and there is no evidence of Shirazi Shia Islam on the coast. Around the 13th century stone buildings began to replace wattle-and-daub. Kilwa exported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, tortoiseshell, coconut oil, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> and gold, and for about 500 years, from about 1100 to 1600, it minted its own coins, which have been found as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>.</p><p>By the 14th century, under the Mahdali dynasty, it was the most powerful city on the Swahili Coast, and in the 15th century the sultanate claimed authority over Malindi, Mombasa, Pemba, Zanzibar, Mafia, Comoro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a> and posts on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited around 1331 and praised the generosity and religion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Hasan ibn Sulaiman, who built the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husuni-kubwa">Husuni Kubwa</a> and a large extension to the Great Mosque. Early in the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> exacted tribute, and in July 1505 a force under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-almeida">Francisco de Almeida</a> besieged and sacked the town; the Portuguese held the island until 1512. In 1784 the Omani rulers of Zanzibar conquered it, and the city was abandoned in the 1840s. It later belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> from 1886 to 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins include the Great Mosque, whose oldest part dates to the 12th century and whose roof once rested on nine hexagonal columns made of single tree trunks, and the palace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husuni-kubwa">Husuni Kubwa</a>, a complex of more than a hundred rooms on a bluff over the ocean, with a pavilion and an octagonal swimming pool. The Gereza Fort stands between the Great Mosque and the Makutani Palace. The island has been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1981, together with the ruins of Songo Mnara, and about 1,150 people live on it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kilwa Kisiwani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Kilwa Kisiwani</b> is a small island off the southern coast of present-day Tanzania, about 300 kilometres south of Dar es Salaam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kilwa-sultanate">Kilwa Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Swahili coast has left a heritage of towns and architecture, from the ruins of <b>Kilwa Kisiwani</b> to the old towns of Lamu and Zanzibar, both World Heritage Sites, where Swahili culture, with its poetry, music, cuisine and carved doors, remains alive.</blockquote><a href="/language/swahili">Swahili · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>On the East African coast Swahili city-states such as <b>Kilwa Kisiwani</b>, Mombasa, Malindi and Pate, Muslim merchant towns under their own rulers, grew rich on the Indian Ocean trade in gold and ivory; Ibn Battuta, who visited in 1331, praised Kilwa as one of the most beautiful towns…</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
